In short
========

   Heroes is similar to the "Tron" and "Nibbles" games of yore, but
includes many graphical improvements and new game features.

   In it, you must maneuver a small vehicle around a world and collect
powerups while avoiding obstacles, your opponents' trails, and even
your own trail.

   Several modes of play are available. Quest is the classical Nibbles,
in Death Match you start with very long tails a must kill your
opponents, in Kill'em All you must run over lemmings moving on the
ground, in Color or Time Cash you must collect money or pyramids of
color.

   Heroes features 12 original soundtracks, 94 levels (in 10 different
tilesets) plus a level editor.  There are five game modes like Quest
(the classical Nibbles), or Kill'em all (run over lemmings).

   Heroes was a DOS game and this is the Unix port.  Presently, the
port is complete, but it needs cleaning before further work (e.g.
network support) can be done.

   Anybody is encouraged to contribute.

License
=======

   The full game (that is: source code, pictures, sound-tracks, and levels)
is distributed under the terms of the GNU General Public License.  See the
file COPYING for details.

Getting Heroes
==============

   The web page of Heroes is `http://www.realtech.scene.org/heroes/'.
This page has been designed by Romuald Genevois
<rgenevois@besancon.net>.  More up to date information about recent
versions are can be found on the Heroes project home page on
SourceForge: `http://sourceforge.net/projects/heroes/'.

   The latest version of Heroes is always uploaded to
`ftp://download.sourceforge.net/pub/sourceforge/heroes/' and announced
on the `heroes-announce' mailing list (*note Mailing lists::).

   Heroes is also uploaded to MetaLab (sunsite) in `/pub/linux/games';
see the MetaLab mirror list
(http://metalab.unc.edu/pub/linux/MIRRORS.html) to find your nearest
mirror. You will have to wait three or four days to have files
available on MetaLab after a release.

   Heroes is distributed as four packages with independent version
numbering.  Be sure to get them four:

`heroes'
     The source code, info file, and man page.

`heroes-data'
     The level data (level maps, sprites, etc.)

`heroes-sound-track'
     The scores for the game (.xm files).

`heroes-sound-effects'
     The sound effects for the game (.wav files).

   The game have been so split because data and sound file are big and
don't evolve as often as the code does.

   A public CVS server is accessible.  See
`http://sourceforge.net/cvs/?group_id=7982' for details.

Needed Libraries
================

   Heroes can be configured to use several libraries to handle display,
sound output and joystick support.

   Here is a list of supported libraries (hopefully you won't have to
install all of them but just a subset).

   * LibMikMod is the only sound engine supported. You don't need it if
     you want to compile Heroes without sound support.  Get it from
     `http://mikmod.darkorb.net/'.  If you install LibMikMod from
     sources you may want to compile LibMikMod without Alsa support
     (*note Troubleshooting::).

   * LibGGI (General Graphic Interface) can be used as a display and
     keyboard driver. Get it from `http://www.ggi-project.org/'.

   * LibGII (General Input Interface) will provide joystick support
     (the game can be compiled without).  Get it from
     `http://www.ggi-project.org/'.  It is needed by LibGGI, by the way.
   * SDL can be used as a display and keyboard driver.  Recent revision
     will also provide support for joysticks.  Get it from
     `http://www.devolution.com/~slouken/SDL/'.

   To summarize, install at least LibGGI or SDL to get video output and
keyboard handling.  Install LibMikMod if you want sound output.  Install
LibGII to get joystick support, unless you have a recent SDL.

   My personal preference goes to LibGGI which seems to be faster than
SDL.  On the other hand, SDL seems better at doing full screen display
and is maintained actively.

   Heroes also support memory debugging library, such as `dmalloc' or
`efence'.  See the `--enable-mem-debug' option below.

configure generic instructions
==============================

  Heroes use autoconf, read INSTALL for generic instructions.  

configure options for Heroes
============================

   You may give several options to `configure' to configure the
building of Heroes.

`--disable-sound'
     Do not link with LibMikMod, thus compiling Heroes without sound
     support.  Sound support is enabled by default, unless LibMikMod is
     not found.

`--disable-joystick'
     Do not include joystick support in the game.  Joystick support is
     enabled by default, unless you don't have GII or joystick support
     in SDL.

`--with-sdl[=DIR]'
     Specify that you want to link with SDL.  This is the default if
     LibGGI is not installed.  If you installed SDL in some
     non-standard place, you can specify the installation path as an
     argument.  For example:
          ./configure --with-sdl=/home/adl/usr
     `configure' will then look for `/home/adl/usr/bin/sdl-config' and
     configure Heroes accordingly.

`--with-GGI[=DIR]'
     Link with LibGGI.  This is the default if LibGGI is installed.  If
     you installed LibGGI in some non-standard place, you can specify
     the installation path as an argument

`--with-libmikmod-prefix[=DIR]'
     Specify where LibMikMod is installed.

`--enable-debug'
     Turn on debugging options: compile and link with debugging symbols,
     enable assertions, and debug code.  This is disabled by default
     because it slow down the code and produce a bigger binary.

`--enable-mem-debug'
     Turn on support for malloc debugging libraries such as electric
     fence or dmalloc (dmalloc will be used if both are installed).
     This is disable by default.

`--disable-user-config'
     Don't use a per-user configuration file, but a system wide file
     instead.  This configuration file record keyboard configuration,
     colors of players, speed of the game, volume, etc. (everything
     that can be configured from the OPTIONS menu of the game).  If you
     save this as a global file, you will want to give special right to
     that file and to the heroes binary so that it can read and write
     that file whoever ran it; this could leads to security issue.

     The default is to save those options in `~/.heroes/options.dat', if
     you specify this option they will be saved in
     `DATADIR/heroes/txt_cfg/options.dat'.

`--disable-user-scores'
     Don't use a per-user scores file, but a system wide file instead.
     If you save this as a global file, you will want to give special
     right to that file and to the heroes binary so that it can read
     and write that file whoever ran it; this could leads to security
     issue.

     The default is to save the scores in `~/.heroes/scores.dat', if
     you specify this option they will be saved in
     `DATADIR/heroes/txt_cfg/scores.dat'.

`--disable-user-saves'
     Don't use a per-user saves file, but a system wide file instead.
     This file allow people to save their progression in Quest Mode.
     Their is 10 save slots.  If you save this as a global file, you
     will want to give special right to that file and to the heroes
     binary so that it can read and write that file whoever ran it;
     this could leads to security issue.

     The default is to save the games in `~/.heroes/savegame.dat', if
     you specify this option they will be saved in
     `DATADIR/heroes/txt_cfg/savegame.dat'.

Reporting bugs, sending suggestions or contribution
===================================================

   We welcome bug reports or suggestions for Heroes (both program or
documentation).  Please email them to <heroes-bugs@lists.sourceforge.net>
or <heroes-discuss@lists.sourceforge.net>.  Please, see the Texinfo 
documentation of Heroes for guidelines regarding bug reporting.
